Move the microtask drain out of processTicksAndRejections and into the C++
callback scope that calls it.

node's tick loop is JS: processTicksAndRejections drains the tick queue, calls
runMicrotasks(), and repeats while ticks remain. On the host JS-engine arm the
microtask checkpoint is one of the two sites where the worker task the guest
occupies can end so the host engine gets a task of its own (the other is
libuv's poll) -- and it cannot end there, because a frame of
processTicksAndRejections is on the stack. Unwinding past a JS frame would
discard a continuation the rewind replay cannot rebuild, so the backend
refuses the turn and finalizers never arrive after a callback.

So the drain happens one frame lower. processTicksAndRejections becomes one
pass over the tick queue; processTicksAgain() is the test that used to be the
loop's condition, asked after the drain (a microtask can enqueue a tick, which
is why it could not move ahead of it) and clearing the two tick flags on its
last answer; and InternalCallbackScope::Close runs the alternation: a pass,
the checkpoint, the test, repeat. The sequence a program observes is
unchanged -- ticks, then microtasks, repeat while ticks remain, then the flags
cleared -- and it is still processTicksAndRejections that runs the ticks.

runNextTicks(), which timers call from JS, keeps a JS-side loop over the same
two functions: it has a JS frame either way, so it was never a yield-legal
site and nothing is gained by moving it.

The second function is registered through setTickCallback's existing binding
rather than a new one, so the two can never be set apart; env_properties.h
gains the one field that holds it.

The patch is arm-neutral: it moves where the drain is called from, not what
is called, and the mozjs arm runs the same sequence it always did.

